millions of families across the nation  are no longer safe from eviction and  housing advocates warn  we could be headed toward a massive wave  of homelessness  moratorium on evictions for federally  backed housing expired last week  it covered more than 12 million  households  but the actual number of people facing  possible evictions  could be much higher adriana diaz spoke  to families in danger of losing their  homes she joins us from chicago good  morning adriana  good morning so renters are now at the  mercy of state laws and just like mask  wearing policies  there's a patchwork of eviction policies  that vary by state  now as we're working on this story one  statistic that really struck us  is that in 17 states black women are  twice as likely as  white renters to be evicted  shamika rollins eight children share two  bedrooms here in richmond virginia  but she's worried about losing their  home after she says she received an  eviction notice  in june first thing i panicked and then  next thing i looked  and i'm like i got my kids and it's like  okay now you got to figure this out  she was behind on rent before the  pandemic grandma is ready  then as kovitt appeared half her income  disappeared when her hours as a home  health aide were  cut her family helped her pay roughly 8  thousand dollars in owed rent but she's  still behind  it's got to be overwhelming i have a lot  of sleepless nights my man is constantly  racing you know what's your next move  catherine azar in colorado works with  show horses but has been out of work  and unable to pay rent since march she  told us she hasn't seen a dollar of  unemployment money  it's a nightmare i don't think anybody  should have to go through this  if she can't get the money together or  work out a deal by today  she'll head to eviction court it's hard  for me  to conceive of  someone being willing to put another  person  out in the street in the middle of a  deadly  pandemic and i'm high risk i'm 70  i have heart 